    "physical_description": "12 folding pages with illustrations and accompanying text of incidents from the Nidana-katha (introduction to the Jataka stories) between a pair of red covers.\r\n\r\nIn various colours with a leaning towards rich blues, greens and reds. \"The style, while evidently owing much to late Indian painting, has markedly national characteristics, which make it unmistakably Burmese. These can be seen in the conventional rendering of a mountain and of architecture, which is treated in a two dimensional way similar to theatrical scenery\". John Lowry, \"Burmese Art\", 1974.",
    "summary": "The volume from which this painting is taken contains illustrations of incidents from the Nidana-katha, which is the introduction to the Jataka stories and may be part of a set of several volumes comprising this introduction.  \r\n\r\nThese stories of the Buddha in his previous incarnations provide examples of moral conduct and socially approved responses to situations. They are thus an unlimited source of themes for literature, art and the theatre. The Nidana-katha and the Jataka stories are popular subjects for Burmese illustrated books, many of which have found their way into European collections. The style, while owing much to late Indian painting, has unmistakable Burmese characteristics, such as the conventional rendering of a mountain and of architecture, which is treated two-dimensionally, in a way suggestive of theatrical scenery.",
    "historical_context": "Gifted by Gen.Sir Euan Miller.",
